# CODEOWNERS — who must review what.
#
# REPO-BASELINE.md §1: the failure this prevents is "security-relevant paths merged
# without the right reviewer". Ownership is stated per path rather than repo-wide so
# that adding a second maintainer later is a one-line change per area, not a rewrite.
#
# Syntax: last matching pattern wins. The catch-all is therefore first.

# ─── Everything ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*                               @konradcinkusz

# ─── The eval contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The spec, the scenarios and the rubrics are the specification this repository
# exists to demonstrate. A change to any of them is a change to what "working"
# means, and is reviewed as such — never as a test-data tweak.
/docs/SPEC.md                   @konradcinkusz
/evals/                         @konradcinkusz
/evals/scenarios/               @konradcinkusz
/evals/rubrics/                 @konradcinkusz

# Baselines record measured state. A hand-edited baseline is how a regression gets
# merged as an "expected change", so this path is called out on its own.
/evals/baselines/               @konradcinkusz

# ─── Agent definition and prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────
# A prompt edit is a behaviour change with no code diff (AI-EVALS.md §2). These
# paths also drive eval re-runs in change detection (.github/workflows/, Phase 6).
/agents/                        @konradcinkusz
/prompts/                       @konradcinkusz

